{
  "note": [
    "Premium and Plus are the names of the paid tiers, not words to be translated.",
    "Left to a translator they come back as the insurance sense: Czech Pojistne,",
    "German Pramie, Greek Asfalistro, Arabic ghali (expensive), Xhosa 'this monthly",
    "payment'. Plus becomes a conjunction: Maltese 'barra minn hekk', Slovak 'navyse',",
    "Tamil 'melum', Korean a grammatical particle.",
    "",
    "So the tier names are kept in English where the script is Latin, and written the",
    "way that language writes them where the script differs. Free is a real word and",
    "is translated, but only in the free-of-charge sense: not German 'Frei' or Czech",
    "'Uvolnit', which mean unoccupied and to release.",
    "",
    "byLanguage also carries labels a translator simply got wrong. Withdraw came back",
    "as the retract sense nearly everywhere (German 'Zurueckziehen', Japanese",
    "'tekkai suru', Russian 'Otzyvat'), when the category means taking cash out of an",
    "account. Recharge came back as Hindi 'phir se daam lagaana', to price again. And",
    "Korean rendered Flowers as the word for menstruation."
  ],

